===== conversations:followup (59s, 7955 chars)
Most replies from Steve will not be a yes or a no. They will be a question, a deflection, or silence. Your job is to stay curious, keep it human, and move him one small step forward.

### When they reply

**They say: Yes, interested**

```text
Good to hear. Let me ask you a quick one before we go any further. Is the main thing you are looking at right now the margin on jobs, getting yourself off site a bit more, or something else entirely? Want to make sure I point you in the right direction.
```

_Do not pitch the system yet. Find out what is actually bothering him. One question. Wait for the answer._

**They say: What is it exactly?**

```text
Good question. In plain terms, I work with MDs in groundworks and civils to find out where the margin is going and fix it. We look at five areas of the business: leads, conversion, what you charge, repeat work, and the margin on jobs. Most owners are losing money in one or two of those without realising it. There is a 3-minute scorecard called the BOSS Scorecard that shows you where your business scores and which area is the weakest link right now. That is usually the best place to start. Want me to send you the link?
```

_Plain language. No system jargon. Move him toward the scorecard, not a sales call._

**They say: How much is it?**

```text
Straight answer: the coaches I work with invest anywhere up to £50,000 a year at the top end. But most owners start for under £1,000 a month, and the first step is just a conversation to see if there is anything worth working on. Before we talk money, it is worth knowing whether this is even the right fit. Can I ask: what is the main thing you are trying to sort in the business right now?
```

_Use this anchor when the price question comes in early. Redirect to their situation before any further numbers. Do not apologise for the investment._

**They say: Not right now**

```text
Fair enough, Steve. No pressure at all. Can I ask what would need to change for the timing to be right? Just good to know, so if I come back to you at some point I am not wasting your time.
```

_One question. No follow-up pitch. If he answers, note it and set a 90-day reminder. If he does not, move him onto the light nurture rhythm._

**They say: We are doing fine**

```text
That is good to hear. Most of the MDs I speak to are doing fine on turnover. The question that usually gets them is whether the margin is where it should be for the hours and risk going in. If the answer is yes, brilliant, you are in better shape than most. Just out of curiosity, are you happy with what the business is putting in your pocket relative to what you are putting in?
```

_Do not argue with fine. Ask one gentle question that invites reflection. If he bats it away again, leave it. Put him in the nurture rhythm._

**They say: Who are you?**

```text
Good question and fair enough to ask. I am Pam Woodford. I started my first business at 17 and spent 28 years building and running businesses across service and retail before I moved into coaching. Four years running, 2015 to 2018, I was ranked the world's number one business coach. For the last 8 years I have worked specifically with MDs in construction and allied trades across Yorkshire. I know the trade well enough to know what a 6am plant breakdown feels like. That is not a metaphor. Happy to have a straight conversation if you want to find out more.
```

_Lead with real proof. Keep it short. Do not list credentials like a CV. One or two sentences of biography, then back to them._

**They send a neutral one-liner (for example: 'OK' or 'Interesting')**

```text
Glad it caught your eye. Quick question: is the margin on jobs the main thing you are looking at right now, or is it more about getting yourself a bit further out of the day-to-day?
```

_Neutral is not no. Treat it as mild interest and ask one focused question. Do not send a wall of text._

**They say: Send me some info**

```text
Happy to. Rather than send you a brochure that ends up in a folder, let me point you at something more useful. There is a 3-minute scorecard called the BOSS Scorecard. It gives you a score out of 100 and shows you which part of your business is quietly capping the profit. Most MDs find it either confirms what they suspected or surprises them. Here is the link: {assessment_url}. No sign-up, no sales call booked automatically at the end. If anything comes up that is worth talking through, I am here.
```

_Send the scorecard link instead of a PDF. It is more useful and gives Pam a natural reason to follow up once he has a score._

### Avoid

- Sending a long pitch when he asks a short question. Match the length of his reply.
- Asking two questions at once. One question. Wait for the answer.
- Treating neutral as a no. It rarely is.
- Jumping to cost before you know what he actually wants to fix.
- Apologising for what you charge. State it plainly and move on.
- Sending a PDF or brochure when the scorecard does a better job.
- Going quiet after a 'not right now'. That is when the nurture rhythm starts.
- Using words like journey, transformation or mindset in a reply to an MD who prices groundworks. It ends the conversation.

### VIP nurture: top 100

**INSIGHT message**

```text
Hi {first_name}, I noticed you are running a groundworks operation in Yorkshire. One thing keeps coming up with MDs I talk to in the trade at the moment: turnover is holding up, the lads are busy, but the margin at the end of the month does not reflect the graft going in. The culprit is almost never where the owner thinks it is. Usually it is sitting in job costing, how repeat work gets handled, or the pricing on smaller contracts that feel like easy wins but quietly eat the margin. I have a 3-minute scorecard that shows you exactly which part of your business is the weakest link right now. No waffle, just a score out of 100 and a straight explanation of what it means. Worth 3 minutes of your time? I can send the link.
```

_Send as a LinkedIn direct message or email. Personalise the opening line with something specific you noticed about their business: a recent contract win, a job they posted about, or a trade body they belong to. Do not send without that specific detail._

> **Who goes on the top 100 list:** An MD or founder of a groundworks, civils, drainage, or allied trades business in Yorkshire or the Humber, turning over £1M to £8M, with 12 to 35 staff. Prioritise anyone who has posted recently about margin pressure, hiring problems, or being stretched thin on site. Also prioritise connections of connections: if a trusted MD in the trade is already in your network, their peers are warm prospects. Review and refresh the list every 90 days. Remove anyone who has been through the full sequence and explicitly said no.

### Nurture rhythm

- **Week 2:** Share a useful insight. Send a LinkedIn post or short message on one of the five profit levers, written in plain language for a construction MD. For example: why margin disappears between winning a job and finishing it, and one thing that fixes it. No pitch. Just useful.
- **Week 5:** Send the BOSS Scorecard. Message him directly. Keep it short. 'I have a 3-minute scorecard that shows which part of your business is capping profit right now. Most MDs either confirm what they suspected or get a surprise. Worth a look? Here is the link: {assessment_url}.' Nothing else.
- **Week 8:** Share a relevant piece of proof or a trade observation. Something specific to construction or allied trades. A pattern you see in job costing, payment terms, or team accountability. If you have a client result with a number by this point, use it here. If not, use a sharp observation from your 8 years in the trade. One short paragraph. No ask.
- **Day 90:** Check-in message. Send on a Tuesday or Wednesday morning. Keep it to three sentences. No links, no scorecard, no pitch. Just a human check-in. If he replies, the conversation restarts.

===== offer:direction (51s, 7068 chars)
I help MDs of groundworks and civils businesses in Yorkshire get more margin out of the jobs they are already winning, build a team that stops bringing every problem to them, and get their time back, without packing in the business they have spent years building, using a proven profit method built on 43 years of running real businesses.

### The gap

| Area | Today | What they want |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Profit and margin | Turnover looks fine but there is nothing in the bank at month-end. Margin gets eaten by rework and underquoting and he cannot see where it went. | Jobs that land the margin he priced. Profit that shows up in the account. Numbers he can actually explain. |
| Cash and pipeline | Three sites running, good activity, but cash is tight. Two tenders sitting unpriced because he was sorting a plant breakdown instead. | Cash that flows predictably. A pipeline he built on purpose, not whatever work came in while he was on site. |
| Team and decisions | He hired a contracts manager to take the pressure off and still does half the job himself because it is quicker. Everything lands on his desk. | A team that owns their patch, makes decisions, and comes to him with solutions. A business that does not stall the moment he steps back. |
| His own life | Missed the match again. His partner has stopped saying much about it, which is worse. No holiday where the phone stays quiet. | A proper weekend. A holiday he actually switches off on. A business worth the graft he has put into it. |

### Why they will buy

- Know-how: Steve suspects coaches have never had a plant breakdown at 6am or chased a main contractor for 90-day payment. Pam has run businesses for 43 years. She knows what the trade actually looks like from the inside.
- Speed: He has tried the seminar, came back fired up, changed nothing within a fortnight. A working relationship with someone who follows up and holds him to account gets to results faster than anything he can do alone.
- Ease: The BOSS Scorecard takes three minutes and shows exactly which part of the business is capping profit. The work starts with his numbers and his jobs. He does not have to figure out where to start.
- Less stress: Right now every problem lands on him. The whole point of the work is to stop that. Most owners find the first quick wins free up hours within the first few weeks, which makes the rest feel manageable rather than one more thing on the list.

### Key messages

1. If your turnover is decent but you cannot explain where the money went, the margin is leaking somewhere between winning the job and finishing it. That is a fixable problem, not just how construction works.
2. The business needing you for every decision is not a sign that you are indispensable. It is a sign that the team and the systems are not yet built to hold the weight. That can be sorted.
3. Busy and profitable are not the same thing. If you are working harder than you were five years ago and the margin is tighter, something in the way the business runs needs to change, not you working more hours.
4. A month where cash is tight despite good turnover is usually one of five things, all of them fixable. The question is which one it is and how quickly you can close it.
5. You have put too much into this business to still be the one sorting everything. The work you carry personally right now is the ceiling on what the business can ever become.

