**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: Busy but skint? Here's why the money keeps disappearing.

Honest question.

You're putting decent numbers through. The lads are busy. The jobs are on. So why is there nothing in the bank at month-end?

I hear this every week from MDs across Yorkshire. Not just once. Every single week. Turnover looks fine. Bank account doesn't agree. And they cannot explain the gap.

Here's what's usually happening.

The margin gets priced at one number and delivered at a different, lower one. Rework, underquoting on groundworks scope, plant hire that runs longer than it should, a variation that never got properly priced in. It happens on almost every job. Each one looks small. Add them up across a month and the profit that should be in the account simply isn't.

The second thing is timing. You invoice at practical completion but payroll lands on the 25th regardless. Main contractors stretch to 60 or 90 days because they can. So you are funding their business with your cash while your own bank balance gives you a headache.

Neither of those is a sign the business is broken. Both of them are fixable.

The one thing to do this week.

Pull the last three jobs you completed. Not the ones still on site. Finished ones. Compare what you priced against what you actually made. Not the invoice value. What landed in the account after you paid out wages, plant, materials and subbies.

Most MDs who do this find one of two things. Either the margin is consistently thinner than priced, which means something in the pricing or delivery needs looking at. Or one job went wrong and dragged everything down, which is a different problem with a different fix.

You cannot sort what you cannot see. That one exercise usually shows you exactly where to look.

