1. **Lock the LinkedIn profile and the BOSS Scorecard link.** Update the headline and about section to reflect the groundworks and civils positioning. Test the BOSS Scorecard link from a mobile browser. If either is not working or not live by end of day two, nothing else starts. The profile is the landing page every prospect checks before they reply.
2. **Build the first prospect list.** Use Sales Navigator or a manual LinkedIn search to pull 150 to 200 Yorkshire-based MDs and directors in groundworks, civils, drainage, plant hire or allied trades, with 12 to 50 employees. Save names, company, and LinkedIn URL in a simple spreadsheet. Filter hard on location and company size. A tight list of the right people outperforms a loose list of thousands.
3. **Send the first 20 connection requests today.** Use the connection message from the voice section of this blueprint. No pitch. No links. First name only. Keep it to three sentences maximum. The goal of the message is one thing: get the connection accepted so the follow-up conversation can start. Track who accepts in the spreadsheet and follow up within 48 hours of each acceptance.

> **Most likely constraint: conversations stalling before a call is booked:** Pam's strongest asset is her credibility with construction MDs: 43 years in business, 8 years in the trade, and a direct Yorkshire voice that Steve recognises as real. The risk is not getting the first message wrong. It is the follow-up. Most campaigns stall because a prospect replies with mild interest and the follow-up is either too slow, too salesy, or drops a link to a calendar before any rapport is built. Watch for this pattern: reply received, follow-up sent with a calendar link, conversation goes cold. That is the signal. The fix is one more conversational message before any call is suggested. Ask a specific question about their business before you offer anything. If replies are coming in but calls are not booking, that is the constraint to address first.
